<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ernest Renan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1823–1892</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ernest Renan was a French scholar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semitic-languages">Semitic languages</a>, historian of religion and essayist. In 1860–1861 he led a French archaeological mission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> that explored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> and Sidon, published as Mission de Phénicie. His Life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> of 1863 caused a sensation by treating Jesus as a historical man, and his lecture What Is a Nation? of 1882 remains a classic text on nationhood.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ernest-renan.jpg" alt="Ernest Renan" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antoine Samuel Adam-Salomon · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ernest_Renan_1876-84.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
